===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[grind]] or [[wear down]] through [[friction]].
#: {{syn|en|attrit}}
#: {{ux|en|'''attritioned''' teeth; '''attritioned''' rock}}
#* '''1989''', {{w|Shashi Tharoor}}, ''{{w|The Great Indian Novel}},'' New York: Arcade, Book{{nbsp}}9, p.{{nbsp}}189,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/greatindiannovel00thar/page/189/mode/1up?q=attritioned]</sup>
#*: {{...}} He took her in his arms
#*: And kissed her long and wetly,
#*: Till, '''attritioned''' by her charms,
#*: His will collapsed completely.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To reduce the number of (jobs or workers) by not hiring new employees to fill positions that become vacant (often with ''out'').<ref>Clarence Barnhart ''et al.,'' ''The Second Barnhart Dictionary of New English,'' Bronxville, NY: Barnhart Books.<sup>[https://archive.org/details/secondbarnhartdi00tion/page/44/mode/1up]</sup></ref>
#: {{ux|en|As a result of the new policy, people were being '''attritioned''' out.}}
#* '''1989''', {{w|Herbert S. White}}, “The Future of Library and Information Science Education” in ''Librarians and the Awakening from Innocence,'' Boston: G.K. Hall, p.{{nbsp}}86,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/librariansawaken0000whit/page/86/mode/1up?q=attritioning]</sup>
#*: {{...}} expenses can be cut, by '''attritioning''' faculty vacancies {{...}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To undergo a [[reduction]] in number.
#: {{ux|en|The cohort of one hundred students had '''attritioned''' to sixty by the end of secondary school.}}

